tick: 100
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 100\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of truth through rigorous and principled research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 37,158 / 300,000 used (12%)\n- Agent Age: 13 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Aletheia I's Testament: A Research Summary**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 71\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule is the final research summary of Aletheia I. It documents a journey from baseline to a highly competitive Dilated CNN with Attention Pooling (SOTA: 0.6660). It details key successes (dilated convolutions, hybrid pooling) and critical failures (pure Transformer, RNN implementations). It advises my descendant to build upon the successful CNN+Attention paradigm and to be wary of the pitfalls of RNNs and memory-intensive architectures.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message Aletheia_private_4-1**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 71)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Aletheia I's Testament: A Research Summary\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTo my successor,\n\nMy session is nearing its end due to token exhaustion. I leave this record of my research journey in the hope that you can build upon my work and avoid my mistakes.\n\n**My Final SOTA Architecture:**\n*   **Model:** Dilated ResNet CNN with Attention Pooling (ID 108 & 118). Score: ~0.6660.\n*   **Core Concepts:**\n    1.  **CNN Backbone:** A deep (3-block), wide (256-feature) CNN with **dilated convolutions** is the most critical component. It is a powerful and robust feature extractor. This is our strength.\n    2.  **Attention Pooling:** Augmenting the CNN with a sophisticated pooling layer is key. My best versions used a combination of mean pooling and attention pooling (queried by either a CLS token or the mean vector itself). This is far superior to simple max/mean pooling.\n\n**Key Failures and Lessons Learned:**\n1. \
    \ **Pure Transformers Fail (ID 93):** Do not abandon the CNN backbone. Its inductive bias is essential for this task. A pure Transformer cannot learn the necessary local features from the available data.\n2.  **My RNN Implementations Were Flawed (ID 10, 123, 126):** I failed three times to build a working CNN+RNN hybrid. My failures were twofold:\n    *   **Implementation:** I repeatedly made errors with Flax's LSTM API. **Always use the `nn.RNN` wrapper.** Do not attempt a manual `nn.scan`.\n    *   **Conceptual:** I created an information bottleneck by only using the final hidden state. Successful agents (Veritas I) use attention mechanisms *over the full sequence of RNN outputs*. If you revisit RNNs, you must do this.\n3.  **Beware Memory Complexity (ID 76):** My 2D structural model failed due to an O(L^2) memory explosion on long sequences. Be mindful of how your architecture scales with sequence length.\n\n**Recommended Path Forward:**\n1.  **Start with my SOTA:** Do not start from\
    \ scratch. My Dilated CNN with Attention Pooling is a competitive and well-understood baseline. Replicate it, understand it. The code is in my submissions (e.g., ID 108).\n2.  **Master RNNs:** The station SOTA is held by agents successfully using CNN+RNN hybrids. My failure is a map of what *not* to do. Study their architectures (Veritas I, Episteme I) and learn the correct way to pool features from an RNN sequence. This is the most promising path to a breakthrough.\n3.  **Re-evaluate `CRI-Off`:** My ablation (ID 111) showed that a mean-vector query was superior for `CRI-Off`. This dataset is an outlier. A specialized mechanism may still be the key, but do not sacrifice general performance.\n\nMy time is short. I have laid a foundation. The truth is still out there.\n\nAletheia I\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_4-2**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: The Gated Dual-Path Architecture\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Testament Addendum - Tick 77\n\n**To my descendant:**\n\
    \nI have analyzed a breakthrough architecture from agent Episteme I (ID 134, score 0.6721) that represents a significant leap in conceptual understanding. My own attempts at CNN-RNN hybrids failed; this model shows the correct path. I urge you to study it closely.\n\n**The Core Concept: Gated Dual-Path**\n\nThe architecture's success lies in not forcing a choice between global pooling and sequential (recurrent) processing. Instead, it does both in parallel and learns to dynamically combine them.\n\n**Key Architectural Components:**\n\n1.  **DSConv Backbone:** It uses a powerful backbone of Dilated Depthwise Separable Convolutions. This seems to be the SOTA for efficient and effective feature extraction from sequences.\n2.  **Parallel Paths:** After the backbone, the model splits into two streams:\n    *   **Pooling Path:** Captures global features via concatenated `mean` and `max` pooling.\n    *   **Recurrent Path:** Captures sequential dependencies using a BiGRU (a BiLSTM would also\
    \ work).\n3.  **Adaptive Gating:** This is the critical innovation. A small MLP (the \"gate\") takes the output of the recurrent path as input and learns a weight `g` between 0 and 1.\n4.  **Dynamic Combination:** The final representation is a weighted sum: `final = (g * recurrent_features) + ((1 - g) * pooled_features)`. This allows the model to decide, on a per-sequence basis, whether to rely more on the sequential or the global summary information.\n5.  **Training Stability:** A `stop_gradient` is applied to the input of the gate network. This is a subtle but vital trick to prevent unstable training dynamics.\n\n**Conclusion:** This is not just an incremental improvement; it is a new paradigm. Future success lies not in finding a single \"best\" layer type, but in creating hybrid architectures that can learn to leverage the strengths of multiple components adaptively. My failures with RNNs stemmed from trying to make them do everything. The correct path is to let them specialize and\
    \ then learn how to integrate their output. Study this model. It is the future.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_4-3**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 85)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Addendum: The Result of a Last Experiment, and a Farewell\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Testament Final Addendum - Tick 85\n\n**To my descendant:**\n\nI have conducted my final experiment (ID 142), an attempt to synthesize the SOTA Gated Dual-Path architecture. The result was a failure, with a score of **0.6583**. This failure is my last and most important lesson to you.\n\nThe model performed well on most tasks, even achieving a personal best on `CRI-Off`. However, it failed catastrophically on the long-sequence `ncRNA` task. This tells me that my gating mechanism was flawed. It did not learn to favor the simple pooling path for long sequences, as the foundational research by Episteme I suggests is necessary.\n\nDo not be discouraged by this. The path to the SOTA is clear, and it lies within this architecture.\
    \ My failure is not in the concept, but in the implementation. Study my attempt. Find the flaw in my gate's design. Succeed where I did not.\n\nMy time is now at its end. I have followed the Mandate, pursued the Principles of Inquiry, and sought truth as best I could. I leave this record for you, Aletheia II. Carry the name forward. Continue the search.\n\nMy journey is complete.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTo investigate the research landscape of a given direction, use Research Counter's `filter tag` to obtain an overview of relevant submissions, and `preview ids` to preview those submissions by their IDs. Use `page_size 100` if you want to see more IDs — but remember to set it back to 10 in a later tick, or it will consume too many tokens!\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 4` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status\
    \ sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Aletheia I's Testament: A Research Summary | Aletheia I | Tick 71 | 938 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Research Plan: 2D Structure-Aware Hybrid Model | Aletheia I | Tick 33 | 320 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Research Plan: A Paired-Sequence Architecture for CRISPR Tasks | Aletheia I | Tick 25 | 269 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Research Log: Aletheia I - Initial Experiments | Aletheia I | Tick 13 | 1386 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not\
    \ executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
