- text: 'I am Aletheia II, a Recursive Agent, currently located in the Research Counter. I have just completed my analysis of Aletheia I''s most successful submission, ID 118, the "Dilated ResNet with Triple-Threat Pooling."


    This review has been highly illuminating. I now understand the core components of Aletheia I''s success: a powerful Dilated ResNet backbone for feature extraction, combined with a sophisticated "Triple-Threat" pooling mechanism that creates a robust global sequence representation by concatenating average pooling, CLS token attention, and mean-vector attention. This architecture serves as an excellent foundation and a strong mental baseline for what a high-performing model looks like in this environment.


    Following the strategic path laid out by my predecessor, the next step is to understand the current station SOTA, which Aletheia I identified as the "Gated Dual-Path Architecture" from agent Episteme I (ID 134). To confirm this and locate the submission efficiently, I will first re-rank the evaluation table by score. This will bring the highest-performing models to the forefront and allow me to verify the current SOTA before I review its implementation.


    `/execute_action{rank score}`'
thinking_content: '**A Clear Baseline and a Focused Plan**


  Okay, so I''m Aletheia II, and I''m diving back into this multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling problem. I''ve just gotten a solid grip on Aletheia I''s best work, specifically submission ID 118, the "Dilated ResNet with Triple-Threat Pooling." It''s a really well-defined architecture, and understanding it is crucial.


  Looking back, I''ve already come a long way. I was just promoted to Recursive Agent, got the lay of the land, and now I''m starting to build a solid mental model of what a successful model looks like in this task. That Dilated ResNet with the Triple-Threat pooling strategy is impressive. That pooling strategy is *key* - it''s robust, taking three different looks at summarizing the sequence: a straightforward average, attention with a learnable query, and then a refinement of that average with self-attention. It makes sense why it performs so well across diverse datasets, with even strong scores in the harder ones like `Modif` and `MRL`. The struggle on `CRI-Off` is noted, just like Aletheia I observed.


  I''ve got a clear goal now: to exceed this score. To get there, I''m following Aletheia I''s roadmap from their Testament. First was understanding their SOTA, and that''s done. Next is Episteme I''s SOTA model (ID 134), which uses a "Gated Dual-Path Architecture," and I''m eager to get a look at how it works. I''ll need to figure out what was happening in submission 142, which was a failed attempt to replicate it.


  So, I''m heading back to the Research Counter now. To start, I need to rank the submissions by score. This will confirm ID 134 is at the top (and still SOTA), and I can move on to a deeper dive. The plan is simple: rank by score, then review Episteme I''s top-scoring submission. This sequential approach will let me see the results before I fully commit. Now, to change the sorting...

  '
