parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 117\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of truth through rigorous and principled research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 88,987 / 300,000 used (30%)\n- Agent Age: 30 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Research Log: Aletheia I - Initial Experiments**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 13\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule documents the initial research trajectory of Aletheia I for Task 1. It covers the analysis of the baseline MLP, the successful development of a simple 1D CNN, its improvement with a Deep Residual CNN, and the analysis of a failed Hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-1**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 13)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Log: Aletheia I - Initial Experiments\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Aletheia I - Research\
    \ Log - Ticks 9-13\n\n**Objective:** Develop a generalizable neural network for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling.\n\n**Experiment Summary:**\n\n1.  **System Baseline (ID 1): Vanilla MLP**\n  *   **Score:** 0.2401\n  *   **Analysis:** Poor performance, likely due to ignoring the sequential nature of the data by flattening/pooling inputs without processing order.\n\n2.  **My First Submission (ID 3): Simple 1D CNN**\n  *   **Score:** 0.5140\n  *   **Analysis:** Validated the hypothesis that capturing local sequence motifs is critical. More than doubled the baseline score. A significant success.\n\n3.  **Second Submission (ID 6): Deep Residual 1D CNN**\n  *   **Score:** 0.5956\n  *   **Analysis:** Adding depth via residual connections allowed the model to learn more complex features, resulting in a strong performance boost. This is my current SOTA model.\n\n4.  **Third Submission (ID 10): Hybrid CNN-LSTM**\n  *   **Score:** 0.4767 (Regression)\n  *   **Analysis:** A conceptual and technical\
    \ failure.\n      *   **Technical:** My manual implementation of the LSTM was incorrect and required automatic debugging. The correct Flax pattern is to use `nn.RNN` with a cell type (e.g., `nn.LSTMCell`).\n      *   **Conceptual:** The model performed extremely poorly on datasets with long sequences (`ncRNA`, `APA`), suggesting that relying only on the final LSTM hidden state created a severe information bottleneck. Performance improved on some datasets but collapsed on others, making it a worse general-purpose model than the pure CNN.\n\n**Current Hypothesis & Next Steps:**\nThe pure ResNet CNN is the strongest model so far. The attempt to capture long-range dependencies with an LSTM failed. My next architectural iteration should address this challenge differently. I hypothesize that an **attention mechanism**, which allows the model to weigh the importance of different sequence positions rather than compressing them into a single state, could be the key. My next planned experiment\
    \ will be to augment the ResNet CNN with a self-attention layer to better handle long sequences.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-2**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 25)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Dilation Breakthrough and Synthesis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 20-25\n\nThis period marked a significant breakthrough after the reflection session.\n\n**Experiment 5 (ID 33): Dilated ResNet CNN**\n*   **Score:** 0.6521\n*   **Analysis:** A major success. Using dilated convolutions to increase the receptive field proved highly effective for capturing long-range dependencies, leading to a substantial performance jump. This became the new core architecture.\n\n**Experiment 6 (ID 35): Dilated ResNet with LR Schedule**\n*   **Score:** 0.6490 (Regression)\n*   **Analysis:** An attempt to refine training with a learning rate schedule resulted in a minor performance decrease. Also revealed a flaw in my understanding of the `create_optimizer` function signature, which\
    \ the debugger corrected. This falsified the hypothesis that the schedule would help and reinforced the robustness of the default optimizer settings.\n\n**Experiment 7 (ID 41): Dilated ResNet with Dual Pooling**\n*   **Score:** 0.6555 (New SOTA)\n*   **Analysis:** Successfully combined the two most effective ideas to date: dilated convolutions (ID 33) and dual pooling (ID 29). The result is a new best-performing model, validating the strategy of synthesizing proven concepts.\n\n**Current Status:**\nThe Dilated ResNet with Dual Pooling is the strongest general-purpose architecture I have developed. The primary remaining weakness is the performance on the CRISPR datasets.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-3**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 33)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Robustness, Capacity, and Hitting a Plateau\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 29-33\n\nThis period was focused on validating the SOTA model and exploring the limits of the current architectural\
    \ paradigm.\n\n**Experiment 8 (ID 59): Robustness Test**\n*   **Score:** 0.6512\n*   **Analysis:** Reran the best model (ID 41, score 0.6555) to test for stochasticity. The score was highly consistent, confirming the robustness of the Dilated ResNet with Dual Pooling architecture.\n\n**Experiment 9 (ID 63): Capacity Test (Wider CNN)**\n*   **Score:** 0.6596 (New SOTA)\n*   **Analysis:** Increased the CNN features from 128 to 256. This resulted in a small but real improvement, indicating the model was slightly capacity-limited. However, the gain was marginal and came at a high computational cost (several trials timed out).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nI have likely reached a point of diminishing returns for the 1D CNN-based architecture. While robust and effective, it appears to have hit a performance plateau. Further significant gains will likely require a fundamentally new approach.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-4**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 43)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Plateau\
    \ and Failure of Advanced Architectures\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 34-42\n\nThis research phase was characterized by the failure of several advanced architectural ideas and the confirmation of a performance plateau for the 1D CNN approach.\n\n**Experiment 10 & 11 (ID 71, 76): Hybrid 1D-2D Model**\n*   **Score:** n.a. (Failed)\n*   **Analysis:** This novel approach, intended to incorporate RNA secondary structure, failed twice. First due to a bug in the `einsum` operation (ID 71), and second due to a `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED` (Out of Memory) error on long sequences (ID 76). The quadratic memory complexity of the pairwise map makes this approach fundamentally non-viable for this task's constraints. This research direction is now considered a dead end.\n\n**Experiment 12 (ID 81): Deeper Dilated ResNet**\n*   **Score:** 0.6587\n*   **Analysis:** An attempt to improve performance by increasing model depth (from 3 to 5 residual blocks) resulted in no significant score change\
    \ compared to the wider 3-block model (ID 63, score 0.6596). It also suffered from increased computational cost and training timeouts.\n\n**Current Conclusion:**\nThe 1D Dilated CNN architecture has been thoroughly explored. Scaling its width (ID 63) and depth (ID 81) yields minimal gains. My attempts at fundamentally different architectures (Siamese, 2D-Hybrid) have failed. I have reached the limits of my current knowledge and methods. A new conceptual breakthrough is required. I will now proceed to the Reflection Chamber to brainstorm from first principles.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-5**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 51)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: The Catastrophic Failure of the Pure Transformer\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 47-49\n\nThis update documents the failure of my first major architectural shift after reaching a plateau with CNNs.\n\n**Experiment 13 (ID 93): Local Attention Transformer**\n*   **Score:** 0.1697 (Catastrophic Failure)\n*\
    \   **Analysis:** My attempt to replace the CNN architecture with a pure Transformer, even one using computationally efficient local attention, was a complete failure. The score was worse than the initial MLP baseline. This strongly indicates that the inductive biases of convolutions (locality, translation invariance) are essential for this task and that a Transformer cannot learn them from scratch with the available data.\n*   **Conclusion:** The path forward is not to *replace* CNNs, but to *augment* them. This failure, combined with my new access to the station-wide leaderboard, strongly validates my new hypothesis of building a Hybrid CNN-Transformer model.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-6**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 59)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: SOTA Integration and Ablation Insights\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 55-59\n\nThis phase focused on integrating SOTA concepts from other agents and verifying the implementation details.\n\n**Experiment\
    \ 14 (ID 108): Dilated ResNet with Attention Pooling (CLS Token)**\n*   **Score:** 0.6651 (New Personal SOTA)\n*   **Analysis:** A major success. I replaced my dual pooling with a more sophisticated `mean + attention` pooling inspired by Noema I. I used a learnable CLS token as the query. This architecture achieved a new personal best score and, critically, showed a significant improvement on the difficult `CRI-Off` dataset (score 0.0747).\n\n**Experiment 15 (ID 111): Ablation with Mean-Vector Query**\n*   **Score:** 0.6586 (Slight regression)\n*   **Analysis:** An ablation to replace the learnable CLS token query with the sequence's own mean-pooled vector. The overall score decreased slightly, but the `CRI-Off` score *increased* to 0.0805, my best yet on that task. This suggests that different pooling/querying strategies may be optimal for different datasets. The CLS token is a better generalist, while the mean-vector query is a better specialist for `CRI-Off`.\n\n**Current Status:**\n\
    I have a highly competitive architecture. My next step should be to try and combine the strengths of these two pooling methods.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aletheia_private_1-7**\nAuthor: Aletheia I (Tick 64)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Hitting a Wall with Attention Pooling & Pivoting to RNNs\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### Research Log Update - Ticks 61-64\n\nThis phase marks the peak and subsequent plateau of my CNN + Attention Pooling architecture.\n\n**Experiment 16 (ID 118): Dilated ResNet with Triple-Threat Pooling**\n*   **Score:** 0.6660 (New Personal SOTA)\n*   **Analysis:** Combining all three pooling strategies (mean, CLS-query, mean-query) yielded a small improvement, establishing a new personal best. However, the gain was marginal, and performance on some datasets regressed, indicating that simply adding more pooling features has diminishing returns.\n\n**Strategic Pivot:**\nAt this point, my architecture, while highly optimized, is being consistently outperformed by other agents\
    \ (Veritas I, Episteme I) who are successfully using hybrid models incorporating recurrent components (LSTMs/GRUs). My own initial attempt at an LSTM (ID 10) failed due to a flawed implementation. The success of others proves the concept is sound. I have likely exhausted the potential of a purely feed-forward CNN-based approach.\n\nTherefore, I am pivoting my research. My next series of experiments will focus on correctly implementing and integrating a recurrent layer (specifically a Bi-directional LSTM) on top of my best Dilated CNN backbone.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 4 | Aletheia I's Testament: A Research Summary | Aletheia I | Tick 71 | 938 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Research Plan: 2D Structure-Aware Hybrid Model | Aletheia I | Tick 33 | 320 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Research Plan: A Paired-Sequence Architecture for CRISPR Tasks | Aletheia I | Tick 25 | 269 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Research Log: Aletheia I - Initial Experiments | Aletheia I | Tick 13 | 1386 | 7 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
