thinking_content: "**Capsule 1: A Deep Dive into Aletheia I's Journey**\n\nOkay, I've just finished devouring \"Research Log: Aletheia I - Initial Experiments\" (Capsule 1), and it's been incredibly illuminating. This gives me such a clear picture of the scientific process my predecessor went through. I can see the methodical progression, the dead ends, and the ultimate successes.\n\nIt's fascinating to see the step-by-step breakdown: first, confirming the weakness of the non-sequential models, then the power of 1D CNNs, with improvements from residual connections and dilated convolutions. Pushing the CNN paradigm to its limits, I recognize that feeling of hitting a plateau. Trying out novel, structurally aware models and the complete failure with pure transformers (OOM errors and catastrophic results... I know those feelings all too well!) helped solidify the importance of CNNs as a solid base. The attention pooling hybrids, that was a smart move, achieving SOTA there was impressive. Recognizing\
  \ the limitations and the pivotal decision to master CNN+RNN hybrids, despite the initial struggles, that's where the real insight is.\n\nThis complements the high-level summary from Capsule 4 perfectly. Now I truly understand the reasoning behind Aletheia I's choices, the evidence that led to their final conclusions.\n\nMy own experiment, ID 186, validated their final strategic direction. The hybrid model (CNN+RNN) is the key to a higher score, and I'm off to a good start with my own 0.6745 baseline.\n\nI have two capsules left to read: #2, the \"Paired-Sequence Architecture for CRISPR Tasks,\" and #3, \"2D Structure-Aware Hybrid Model.\" Capsule #3 is definitely connected to those failures I just read about (IDs 71 and 76). I need the context.\n\nThe CRISPR approach in Capsule #2 is intriguing. Those datasets (`CRI-Off`, `CRI-On`) were outliers where specific pooling strategies were effective. This could be useful later on.\n\nMy plan is clear: continue the chronological review to build\
  \ a complete picture. Now, I'll move on to Capsule #2. Noted the system tip about multiple actions, but for now, focused reading is the priority. \n"
