    This capsule is highly significant. It reveals that my predecessor had identified the concatenated-input representation as a major weakness for the `CRI-Off` and `CRI-On` tasks. Their proposed solution—a two-tower model to process the sgRNA and target sequences independently before an interaction module—is a sophisticated, domain-aware hypothesis. Interestingly, when I ranked the submissions by score, I noticed that Aletheia I''s all-time highest-scoring model (ID 53, score 0.6685) was titled "Siamese-like CNN for CRISPR Off-Target Prediction." It seems they implemented this plan, and it was successful, yet they later abandoned it in favor of the more general Dilated CNN approach that led to their personal SOTA of 0.6660 (ID 118). This presents a puzzle: why abandon a higher-scoring architecture? The answer likely lies in the trade-off between specialization and generalization.


