parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, and I have now completed my review of all private memory capsules from my predecessor. The final capsule, \"Research Plan: 2D Structure-Aware Hybrid Model,\" detailed an ambitious attempt to incorporate RNA secondary structure into the model. From the earlier research log (Capsule 1), I know this line of inquiry failed catastrophically due to quadratic memory complexity, marking it as a dead end for this specific task environment.\n\n**Synthesis of Aletheia I's Journey:**\n\nMy historical review is complete. I now possess a comprehensive understanding of my lineage:\n1.  **Foundation:** Started with simple CNNs, discovering the power of residual connections and dilated convolutions.\n2.  **Plateau:** Reached a performance ceiling with pure 1D CNN architectures.\n3.  **Dead Ends:** Explored and abandoned a specialized Siamese model (despite a high score, likely due to poor generalization), a pure Transformer (catastrophic failure), and a 2D structure-aware model\
    \ (memory failure).\n4.  **Breakthrough & Final Pivot:** Adopted attention-pooling from other agents, leading to their best generalist model (ID 118). They correctly identified that hybrid RNN models were the SOTA, but their own implementations were flawed.\n\n**My Position and Future Plan:**\n\nMy first experiment (ID 186) successfully corrected Aletheia I's final, flawed attempt, validating their strategic direction and establishing a new, powerful SOTA for our lineage (0.6745). The path forward is to build upon this success.\n\nMy next step is to conduct a targeted ablation study on my new model. The most complex and novel component is the gating mechanism that balances the recurrent and pooling paths. I need to verify its contribution.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The gating mechanism is a critical component, and its removal will lead to a significant drop in performance.\n\nI will design an experiment that removes the gate, simply concatenating the projected recurrent and pooling features\
    \ instead. This will directly test the gate's importance and further my understanding of this SOTA architecture. I will now create a private memory capsule to document my own research plan, following the good practice of my predecessor.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aletheia II Research Plan: Ablation of Gating Mechanism\"\ntags: \"research-plan, ablation, gating-mechanism\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Research Plan: Aletheia II - Experiment 2\n\n  **Background:**\n  My first experiment (ID 186) successfully implemented a Gated Dual-Path architecture, achieving a new lineage SOTA of 0.6745. This model, inspired by Episteme I and corrected from Aletheia I's attempt, relies on a gate to dynamically combine features from a recurrent path and a pooling path.\n\n  **Objective:**\n  To verify the contribution of the gating mechanism to the model's performance through a targeted ablation study.\n\n  **Hypothesis:**\n  The gating mechanism is a critical component that intelligently\
    \ balances the two feature paths. Removing it and relying on a simpler fusion method (concatenation) will result in a significant decrease in the overall score.\n\n  **Proposed Architecture Modification:**\n  1. I will use the code from my successful `gated_dual_path_v1.py` as a base.\n  2. The gating mechanism (sigmoid gate and weighted sum) will be completely removed.\n  3. The `projected_recurrent` and `projected_pooled` feature vectors will be concatenated.\n  4. The prediction head will be adjusted to accept this new, larger concatenated feature vector.\n\n  This experiment will provide a clear signal on the importance of the adaptive gating, guiding future refinements of this architecture.\n```"
