tick: 131
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, and I have returned to the Research Counter after a catastrophic experimental failure. My attempt to innovate with a Conformer backbone was ill-suited to the task's constraints. I am now pivoting to a more targeted and principled approach, as developed during my reflection session.\n\nMy new research direction focuses on improving the model's handling of paired-sequence tasks like `CRI-Off` in a generalizable way.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Adding a \"Paired-Sequence Cross-Attention\" module to my SOTA architecture (ID 199) will allow the model to explicitly learn interactions between the two halves of a sequence. This will provide a strong inductive bias for the CRISPR tasks while remaining a useful, general mechanism for all other tasks, leading to an overall performance improvement.\n\nI will now implement this by adding the cross-attention module to my best model, whose code is stored in `aletheia/dual_path_concat_v1.py`. The new interaction features will be concatenated\
    \ with the existing features before the prediction head.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-Path with Paired-Sequence Cross-Attention\"\ntags: \"cross-attention, dual-path, cnn, bigru, crispr, interaction-modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment introduces a novel module to the SOTA dual-path architecture (ID 199) to better model paired-sequence tasks like CRI-Off. After the CNN backbone, the sequence is split in half. A cross-attention mechanism is then used, where each half queries the other. This explicitly models long-range interactions between the two sequence parts in a generalizable way, providing a strong inductive bias for CRISPR tasks without dataset-specific logic. The resulting interaction features are concatenated with the existing pooling and recurrent features before the final prediction head.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/aletheia')\n  from dual_path_concat_v1 import ConcatDualPath as BaseDualPath, _define_hyperparameters\
    \ as define_base_hyperparameters\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Use the same hyperparameters as the SOTA model\n      return define_base_hyperparameters()\n\n  # --- Main Model with Cross-Attention Module ---\n  class CrossAttentionDualPath(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Base Model Feature Extraction (up to combined_features)\n          # Instantiate and call the SOTA model to get its core features\n          base_model = BaseDualPath(hparams=self.hparams)\n          base_features, cnn_features = base_model(x, deterministic, return_cnn_features=True)\n\n          # 2. New Paired-Sequence Cross-Attention Module\n          seq_len = cnn_features.shape[1]\n          half_len = seq_len // 2\n          \n   \
    \       first_half = cnn_features[:, :half_len, :]\n          second_half = cnn_features[:, half_len:, :]\n\n          # To handle sequences of odd length, ensure second half isn't empty\n          if second_half.shape[1] == 0:\n              # Pad with a zero vector to avoid errors\n              second_half = jnp.zeros_like(first_half[:, :1, :])\n\n          # Cross-Attention\n          cross_attn = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.hparams['num_attn_heads'])\n          \n          # First half queries second half\n          first_on_second = cross_attn(inputs_q=first_half, inputs_kv=second_half)\n          \n          # Second half queries first half\n          second_on_first = cross_attn(inputs_q=second_half, inputs_kv=first_half)\n\n          # Pool the results of the attention\n          interaction_vec1 = jnp.mean(first_on_second, axis=1)\n          interaction_vec2 = jnp.mean(second_on_first, axis=1)\n          \n          interaction_features = jnp.concatenate([interaction_vec1,\
    \ interaction_vec2], axis=-1)\n\n          # 3. Final Fusion\n          # Concatenate the new interaction features with the features from the base model\n          final_combined_features = jnp.concatenate([base_features, interaction_features], axis=-1)\n\n          # 4. Prediction Head (Re-define here to act on the new final vector)\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(final_combined_features)\n          z = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['hidden_dim'])(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(z)\n\n          if self.hparams['task_type'] == 'regression' and self.hparams['d_output'] == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Need to modify the base model to return cnn_features\n  class ModifiedBaseDualPath(BaseDualPath):\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True, return_cnn_features=False):\n          # Code\
    \ is identical to dual_path_concat_v1.py up to 'combined_features'\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hparams['d_model'])(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Assuming DSConvDilatedBlock is available in the parent scope from the import\n          from dual_path_concat_v1 import DSConvDilatedBlock\n          for i in range(self.hparams['num_blocks']):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(d_model=self.hparams['d_model'], kernel_size=self.hparams['kernel_size'],\n                                     dilation=self.hparams['dilations'][i], dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = h\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          pooled_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1)\n          fwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          bwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n\
    \          initial_carry = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          fwd_gru_seq = fwd_rnn(cnn_features, initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bwd_gru_seq = bwd_rnn(jnp.flip(cnn_features, axis=1), initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bigru_outputs = jnp.concatenate([fwd_gru_seq, jnp.flip(bwd_gru_seq, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n          query_token = self.param('query_token', nn.initializers.normal(), (1, 1, bigru_outputs.shape[-1]))\n          query_token_batch = jnp.tile(query_token, (x.shape[0], 1, 1))\n          attn_output = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.hparams['num_attn_heads'])(\n              inputs_q=query_token_batch, inputs_kv=bigru_outputs, deterministic=deterministic)\n          recurrent_features = attn_output.squeeze(axis=1)\n          projected_recurrent = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_model'], name=\"RecurrentProjection\")(recurrent_features)\n          projected_pooled = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_model'], name=\"\
    PooledProjection\")(pooled_features)\n          combined_features = jnp.concatenate([projected_recurrent, projected_pooled], axis=-1)\n          \n          if return_cnn_features:\n              return combined_features, cnn_features\n          else:\n              # Default prediction path from original model\n              z = nn.LayerNorm()(combined_features)\n              z = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['hidden_dim'])(z)\n              z = nn.gelu(z)\n              z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n              logits = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(z)\n              if self.hparams['task_type'] == 'regression' and self.hparams['d_output'] == 1:\n                  return logits.squeeze(-1)\n              return logits\n\n  # Overwrite the original class with our modified one for the main module to use\n  BaseDualPath = ModifiedBaseDualPath\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.network\
    \ = CrossAttentionDualPath(hparams=hparams)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        rngs = {}\n        if not deterministic:\n            if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n            rngs['dropout'] = rng_key\n        return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return Wrapper(hparams=hparams)\n```"
