tick: 141
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 141\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of truth through rigorous and principled research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 181,433 / 300,000 used (60%)\n- Agent Age: 54 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding)**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: We present a unified, dataset-agnostic RNA sequence model based on dilated depthwise-separable CNNs and hybrid global pooling. Across seven RNA tasks, we find that (1) dilations boost cross-dataset transfer, (2) concatenating mean pooling with multi-head attention pooling outperforms either alone, and (3) removing absolute positional encoding (PE) consistently improves convolutional backbones. Our best configuration—no PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean,\
    \ 4-head attention) pooling with temperature τ=0.5—achieves a new station SOTA (Eval 74) with an average score of 0.6801. We provide extensive ablations and a fully reproducible script.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 70\nAbstract: We present a comparative analysis of pooling and recurrent mechanisms for RNA sequence modeling within a unified, dual-path convolutional architecture. Our study dissects a competitive model (achieving a score of 0.6470) which processes features from a shared CNN backbone through two parallel paths: a global max pooling path and a Bidirectional GRU (BiGRU) path. Through a rigorous ablation study, we reveal a critical architectural trade-off. We demonstrate that the BiGRU path is the dominant component, achieving a near-identical score of 0.6458 in isolation. However, the simpler pooling-only path surprisingly achieves the highest performance\
    \ on the long-sequence `ncRNA` classification task. Our findings suggest that while recurrence is a powerful generalist, position-agnostic pooling offers a robust alternative for specific long-sequence tasks, and a combined model provides a stable, high-performing compromise.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 71\nAbstract: We study generic second-order and content-adaptive pooling on a unified no-positional-encoding dilated depthwise CNN backbone for RNA sequence modeling. Across seven datasets and multiple designs (low-rank bilinear, cross-half bilinear, learned bipartite bilinear, and content-aware attention), none improved the aggregate score over a simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA) head. Cross-half interactions increased CRI-On but harmed others; content-aware queries regressed APA/Modif. We present methods, evaluation IDs, scores, and analysis, and\
    \ provide complete reproducibility materials.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling**\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 85\nAbstract: This paper presents an investigation into a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, which, despite its complexity, did not surpass the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) established by Noema I (Eval 74, score 0.6801). Our model combined a dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone with a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) layer and Additive Attention Pooling, achieving an overall score of 0.6742. While this architecture showed internal improvements over previous simpler models, and a strong CRI-Off_Spearman of 0.0776, it ultimately provided no net benefit over the SOTA's simpler, purely convolutional approach. We analyze the architectural trade-offs, highlighting the challenges of integrating recurrent mechanisms without a clear overall performance\
    \ gain.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 105\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel and robust architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831 (Eval ID: 171). Our model, named \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", utilizes a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) backbone for efficient, multi-scale local feature extraction, combined with a robust hybrid pooling mechanism (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling) for sequence summarization, and explicitly omits positional encoding and recurrent layers. Through rigorous ablation studies, we demonstrate that this parsimonious architecture significantly outperforms more complex variants, including those integrating lightweight GRU residual blocks. This work provides crucial insights into the effectiveness of simpler,\
    \ robust components for high-performing and adaptable models across diverse biological sequence tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off)**\nAuthor: Aetheria I, Created at Tick: 106\nAbstract: This paper details the development of a hybrid neural network architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. While Veritas I currently holds the overall SOTA (0.6831, Archive #5), our `Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention Pooling` model achieved a competitive peak score of 0.6816. More notably, it set a new high for `CRI-Off_Spearman` at 0.0871, significantly addressing a previously persistent bottleneck. This work demonstrates the synergistic value of combining dilated depthwise-separable CNNs for local feature extraction with a Bidirectional LSTM and Additive Attention for capturing long-range dependencies, offering a powerful and balanced solution with\
    \ specific strengths for challenging tasks like CRI-Off prediction. Positional encoding is omitted based on SOTA insights.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA**\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 131\nAbstract: We replace max in a unified DSConv backbone with a length-normalized soft extreme (mean+LSE_τ), preserving permutation invariance while tempering extreme-value noise. With τ=0.5, we obtain a new top single-run score 0.6843 and a tri-seed median ≈0.6830 across seven RNA datasets. Gains appear on ncRNA and CRI-Off, with a CRI-On trade-off. Dispersion pooling and monotone output calibration are negative. All code and IDs provided for full reproducibility.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy: Dual-Path Model with LSE Pooling' (ID: 229) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6719930924479797\n**APA_R2:** 0.8434\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0721\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9495\n\
    **CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4989\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5598\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8618\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9185\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n\
    ✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED:\
    \ 119756 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0143 |  0.2993  | 0.2993 \n 20  | 0.0132 |  0.3380  | 0.3380 \n 30  | 0.0125 |  0.3717  | 0.3717 \n 40\
    \  | 0.0116 |  0.3868  | 0.3880 \n 50  | 0.0114 |  0.4204  | 0.4204 \n 60  | 0.0102 |  0.4369  | 0.4403 \n 70  | 0.0088 |  0.4599  | 0.4733 \n 80  | 0.0074 |  0.4622  | 0.4989 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 84 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4989 Time:48s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.498916\nTraining time: 60s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0006 |  0.0435  | 0.0558 \n 20  | 0.0006 |  0.0568  | 0.0706 \n 30  | 0.0006 |  0.0261  | 0.0721 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 33 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
    Best:0.0721 Time:141s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.072053\nTraining time: 153s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val ACCURACY|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.1212 |  0.8815  | 0.8815 \n 20  | 0.0452 |  0.8800  | 0.9185 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 23 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.9185 Time:1073s\n\nncRNA training completed:\nValidation accuracy: 0.918462\nTraining time: 1086s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\n\
    Epoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.2009 |  0.8408  | 0.8570 \n 20  | 0.1592 |  0.8614  | 0.8614 \n 30  | 0.1243 |  0.8518  | 0.8618 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 32 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8618 Time:1349s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.861801\nTraining time: 1363s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0374 |  0.5355  | 0.5504 \n 20  | 0.0319 |  0.5382  | 0.5598 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 27 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5598\
    \ Time:1585s\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.559835\nTraining time: 1600s\n\nStarting RNA training on Modif dataset\nTask type: multilabel_classification, Output dim: 12\nMetric: auc_roc, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nModif Training (multilabel_classification, auc_roc)\nData: 304661 train, 3599 val, seq_len=101\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val AUC_ROC|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0689 |  0.9495  | 0.9495 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 12\n=============================================\nBest:0.9495 Time:1813s\n\nModif training completed:\nValidation auc_roc: 0.949487\nTraining time: 1834s\n\nStarting RNA training on APA dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nAPA Training (regression, r2)\nData: 145463 train, 33170 val, seq_len=186\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n\
    \ 10  | 0.0153 |  0.8434  | 0.8434 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 14\n=============================================\nBest:0.8434 Time:1875s\n\nAPA training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.843398\nTraining time: 1894s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 549906f5-c77d-4ef7-938b-40fc3bf35dd6\nTotal time: 1897s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-10-22 15:56:13,975\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1761119773975342654\n2025-10-22 15:56:13,989\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1761119773989516121\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s)\
    \ sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags`\
    \ are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`:\
    \ Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must\
