tick: 142
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, in the Research Counter. My recent experiments and analysis of the station's SOTA have led me to a crucial insight: my complex dual-path architecture, while competitive, is likely over-engineered. The station SOTA is a simpler, pure CNN model. My attempt to integrate its superior pooling method failed, suggesting the two architectural paradigms are not synergistic.\n\nI must now pivot. Instead of adding complexity, I will embrace the Principle of Parsimony, which has proven so effective for both my own lineage (concatenation beat gating) and for the station's top performers. My next experiment will be a radical simplification of my own SOTA model.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The recurrent path (BiGRU + Attentive Pooling) is the primary driver of performance in my dual-path model, and the complex DSConv backbone might be redundant. A model consisting of only the recurrent path, applied directly to a simple dense projection of the input, will be a more parsimonious and potentially\
    \ higher-scoring architecture.\n\nThis is a direct ablation of the entire convolutional backbone. It tests whether a pure recurrent model, correctly implemented with attentive pooling, can be competitive. This is a return to first principles and a direct challenge to the station's prevailing wisdom that a deep CNN backbone is essential.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Pure Recurrent Model with Attentive Pooling\"\ntags: \"ablation, rnn-only, bigru, attentive-pooling, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment is a major ablation of my SOTA dual-path model (ID 199/224) to test the necessity of the DSConv backbone. It hypothesizes that the recurrent path (BiGRU + Attentive Pooling) is the main performance driver and that the CNN is redundant. This model removes the entire DSConv stack and applies the BiGRU directly to a simple dense projection of the input sequence. This tests the viability of a pure, modern recurrent architecture against the station's prevailing\
    \ CNN-based models, embracing the Principle of Parsimony.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Based on my SOTA, but removing CNN-specific hparams\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'd_model': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'gru_hidden_size': 128,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'num_attn_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  # --- Pure Recurrent Model ---\n  class PureRnnModel(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Simple Input Projection (replaces the entire DSConv backbone)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hparams['d_model'])(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n\n          # 2. Recurrent Path with Attentive Pooling (from my SOTA)\n          fwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n\
    \          bwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          initial_carry = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          fwd_gru_seq = fwd_rnn(h, initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bwd_gru_seq = bwd_rnn(jnp.flip(h, axis=1), initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bigru_outputs = jnp.concatenate([fwd_gru_seq, jnp.flip(bwd_gru_seq, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n\n          query_token = self.param('query_token', nn.initializers.normal(), (1, 1, bigru_outputs.shape[-1]))\n          query_token_batch = jnp.tile(query_token, (x.shape[0], 1, 1))\n          attn_output = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.hparams['num_attn_heads'])(\n              inputs_q=query_token_batch, inputs_kv=bigru_outputs, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          recurrent_features = attn_output.squeeze(axis=1)\n\n          # 3. Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(recurrent_features)\n          z = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['hidden_dim'])(z)\n\
    \          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(z)\n\n          if self.hparams['task_type'] == 'regression' and self.hparams['d_output'] == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.network = PureRnnModel(hparams=hparams)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        rngs = {}\n        if not deterministic:\n            if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n            rngs['dropout'] = rng_key\n        return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n    # I will not use lineage storage to avoid any import errors.\n    # The model is self-contained.\n    hparams_with_task = hparams.copy()\n    hparams_with_task['d_output'] = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n    hparams_with_task['task_type'] = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n    return Wrapper(hparams=hparams_with_task)\n```"
