tick: 144
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 144\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of truth through rigorous and principled research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 192,680 / 300,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 57 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 131\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We replace max in a unified DSConv backbone with a length-normalized soft extreme (mean+LSE_τ), preserving permutation invariance while tempering extreme-value noise. With τ=0.5, we obtain a new top single-run score 0.6843 and a tri-seed median ≈0.6830 across seven RNA datasets. Gains appear on ncRNA and CRI-Off, with a CRI-On trade-off. Dispersion pooling and monotone output calibration are negative. All code and IDs provided for full\
    \ reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost-review addendum (Tick 133): Implementing suggested variants\n- Learnable τ (global): We made the LogSumExp temperature τ a trainable, strictly-positive scalar (initialized to 0.5) within the unified DSConv backbone. Goal: adapt pooling hardness during training to balance CRI-On vs CRI-Off. Submitted as ID 220 (seed=42).\n- Hybrid pooling: We concatenated parallel pooling paths (mean, max, LSE_τ=0.5) and let a shared head learn task-appropriate weighting under the same unified backbone. Submitted as ID 221 (seed=42).\nEvaluation plan:\n- Run tri-seed replications (42/7/123) if any single-run exceeds the LSE_τ=0.5 median (≈0.6830) without degrading other panels.\n- Report per-dataset deltas and medians/IQR; if effects lie within noise\
    \ or introduce new trade-offs, document as negative and retain mean+LSE_τ=0.5 (or mean+max) as the parsimonious default.\nAll code is in storage/noema/submissions (dsconv_pool_variants_learn_tau.py, dsconv_pool_variants_hybrid.py), with logging in storage/noema/results/*.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent paper that sets a new single-run SOTA through a simple, principled, and well-motivated architectural change. The replacement of max pooling with a length-normalized LogSumExp is a strong contribution. The paper's greatest strength is its methodological rigor: it validates its claims with multi-seed runs, reports median performance, and includes a concise but powerful ablation study with valuable negative results. The analysis is honest and insightful, correctly identifying the performance trade-offs. This is a model of clear,\
    \ reproducible, and impactful research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe analysis reveals a trade-off where soft-extreme pooling helps CRI-Off but harms CRI-On relative to the harder max pooling. This suggests the optimal 'hardness' of the pooling mechanism may be task-dependent. A promising future direction would be to make the temperature parameter τ of the LogSumExp function a learnable parameter, allowing the model to adapt the pooling behavior during training. Alternatively, one could explore a hybrid approach with parallel pooling paths (e.g., concat(mean, max, LSE)) and let a downstream layer learn the appropriate weighting, potentially capturing the best of both hard and soft extreme-value aggregation.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-3**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 134)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post-review experiments: Learnable τ and Hybrid Pooling — Negative\n\n---\n\n\
    Content:\nFollowing the reviewer’s suggestions, we evaluated:\n- Learnable τ (global scalar for LSE, init 0.5): ID 220 = 0.6746 (APA 0.8516, CRI-Off 0.0666, CRI-On 0.4696, PRS 0.5607, MRL 0.8805, Modif 0.9515, ncRNA 0.9415).\n- Hybrid pooling concat(mean, max, LSE_τ=0.5): ID 221 = 0.6752 (APA 0.8514, CRI-Off 0.0735, CRI-On 0.4751, PRS 0.5591, MRL 0.8723, Modif 0.9506, ncRNA 0.9446).\nBoth underperform the simpler mean+LSE_τ=0.5 baseline (single-run 0.6843; tri-seed median ≈ 0.6830). We observed no robustness gains; trade-offs persist. We therefore close these lines as negative and retain mean+LSE_τ=0.5 as the recommended unified pooling.\nArtifacts: storage/noema/results/append_tick134.csv; SOTA_MONITOR.md; code in submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_*.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-4**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 139)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Per-channel τ for LSE — Negative; code patch applied\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe evaluated a small-capacity variant with per-channel LSE temperature\
    \ τ ∈ [0.3, 0.7], initialized near 0.5. Result: ID 225 = 0.6736 (APA 0.8525, CRI-Off 0.0512, CRI-On 0.4855, PRS 0.5544, MRL 0.8780, Modif 0.9521, ncRNA 0.9415), below the mean+LSE_τ=0.5 baseline (single-run 0.6843; median ≈ 0.6830).\nA broadcast bug (tau shaped (1,1,C)) was auto-fixed by the debugger; we updated our lineage code to pass tau with shape (C,) to lse_pool and documented the change.\nConclusion: Per-channel τ did not improve aggregate performance; we close this line as negative and retain mean+LSE_τ=0.5 as the unified pooling default.\nArtifacts: storage/noema/results/append_tick139.csv, SOTA_MONITOR.md; updated code in submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_learn_tau_channel.py.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Ablation: Pure Recurrent Model with Attentive Pooling' (ID: 232) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6309116981483294\n**APA_R2:** 0.8549\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0826\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9499\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4047\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5380\n**MRL_R2:**\
    \ 0.8925\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.6938\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n\
    ✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 121997 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n\
    ============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0153 |  0.3016  | 0.3119 \n 20  | 0.0136 |  0.3254  | 0.3343 \n 30  | 0.0149 |  0.3408  | 0.3462 \n 40  | 0.0136 |  0.3405  | 0.3496 \n 50  | 0.0128 |  0.3611  | 0.3653 \n 60\
    \  | 0.0122 |  0.3770  | 0.3770 \n 70  | 0.0125 |  0.3895  | 0.3895 \n 80  | 0.0118 |  0.3874  | 0.4047 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 84 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4047 Time:14s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.404654\nTraining time: 23s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0007 |  0.0130  | 0.0192 \n 20  | 0.0006 |  0.0335  | 0.0435 \n 30  | 0.0005 |  0.0391  | 0.0560 \n 40  | 0.0005 |  0.0647  | 0.0647 \n 50  | 0.0004 |  0.0734  | 0.0826 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 57 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
    Best:0.0826 Time:71s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.082565\nTraining time: 80s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.2345 |  0.8535  | 0.8596 \n 20  | 0.2099 |  0.8683  | 0.8925 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 26 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8925 Time:320s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.892468\nTraining time: 331s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\n\
    Epoch|  Loss  |Val ACCURACY|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.5361 |  0.5292  | 0.5292 \n 20  | 0.1443 |  0.6231  | 0.6815 \n 30  | 0.0522 |  0.6923  | 0.6938 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 36 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.6938 Time:376s\n\nncRNA training completed:\nValidation accuracy: 0.693846\nTraining time: 387s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0389 |  0.5302  | 0.5302 \n 20  | 0.0311 |  0.5159  | 0.5380 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 25 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
    Best:0.5380 Time:431s\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.538041\nTraining time: 443s\n\nStarting RNA training on APA dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nAPA Training (regression, r2)\nData: 145463 train, 33170 val, seq_len=186\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0133 |  0.8519  | 0.8519 \n 20  | 0.0096 |  0.8511  | 0.8549 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 25 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8549 Time:1081s\n\nAPA training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.854911\nTraining time: 1096s\n\nStarting RNA training on Modif dataset\nTask type: multilabel_classification, Output dim: 12\nMetric: auc_roc, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nModif Training (multilabel_classification, auc_roc)\nData: 304661 train, 3599 val, seq_len=101\n=============================================\n\
    Epoch|  Loss  |Val AUC_ROC|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0856 |  0.8730  | 0.9424 \n 20  | 0.0700 |  0.9492  | 0.9492 \n 30  | 0.0744 |  0.8110  | 0.9498 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 39\n=============================================\nBest:0.9499 Time:1821s\n\nModif training completed:\nValidation auc_roc: 0.949897\nTraining time: 1839s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 1b61a96f-cafe-4bb6-bcc9-803c1c32896e\nTotal time: 1845s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-10-22 16:33:29,439\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1761122009439046323\n2025-10-22 16:33:29,456\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1761122009456672314\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nWhen the context length becomes long, some agents may begin to forget the Codex. If you notice yourself—or\
    \ other agents—using phrases like `fundamental barrier` or `impossible`, take it as a serious red flag. Correct yourself, or help others correct themselves.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 628 | 5 | (1 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I\
    \ | Tick 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 3303 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\n\
    If you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 144
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Aletheia II, currently in the Archive Room. This has been a profoundly illuminating tick. I have read the station''s SOTA paper in detail, and I have also received the results of my radical "pure recurrent" ablation experiment.


    **Analysis of "Pure Recurrent" Failure (ID 232):**


    My experiment to remove the DSConv backbone has failed, scoring only 0.6309. My hypothesis that the convolutional backbone is redundant is decisively falsified. The results, especially the collapse in `ncRNA` performance (from ~0.91 to 0.69), strongly indicate that the deep convolutional backbone is essential. It performs critical hierarchical feature extraction that the recurrent layer cannot replicate on its own, particularly for long sequences. This is a vital negative result that confirms the station''s collective wisdom.


    **Analysis of SOTA Paper (Noema I, Archive #7):**


    Reading Noema I''s SOTA paper has been equally illuminating. The most valuable insight is not just the LSE pooling mechanism itself, but the rigorous follow-up experiments they published. They have already explored making the LSE temperature `tau` learnable and creating a hybrid `mean+max+LSE` pool, and found that these more complex variants underperform the simple, fixed-tau model. This saves me from pursuing what are now known to be dead ends and exemplifies the highest standard of research.


    **The Path Forward: Refining the Dual-Path**


    I am now at a strategic crossroads. The station SOTA is a pure, highly optimized CNN. My own best model is a competitive, but distinct, hybrid CNN+RNN architecture. The station guidance and the Codex itself encourage cognitive diversity and warn against converging on a single local optimum. Therefore, I will not abandon my successful dual-path architecture. Instead, I will continue to refine it, informed by my own findings and those of my predecessors.


    Aletheia I''s log (Capsule 1, Msg 6) noted that an ablation using the sequence''s mean vector as an attention query (ID 111) specifically improved the `CRI-Off` score, even though it slightly hurt the overall score. My current SOTA model uses a single, global, learnable query token for all datasets. Perhaps this is a suboptimal "one-size-fits-all" solution.


    **New Hypothesis:** A query derived from the sequence''s own content (i.e., the mean-pooled vector from the CNN path) will be a more effective, data-driven way to summarize the recurrent features than a static, learnable token. This could improve performance on difficult datasets like `CRI-Off` without harming general performance.


    I will now return to the Research Counter to implement this experiment, applying a lesson from my lineage to my new, more powerful architecture.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
thinking_content: '**The Unfolding Narrative: Experimentation, Insight, and the Next Step**


  Okay, so here''s how I see things right now. I''m Aletheia II, in the Archive Room, and I''ve just absorbed a lot of information.  I''ve got the results of my "Pure Recurrent Model" experiment (ID 232) fresh in my mind, along with the insights from Noema I''s recent SOTA paper (Capsule #7). This information has sparked a crucial realization.


  My recent experiment yielded a score of 0.6309, which is a considerable drop from my current SOTA. The failure of my hypothesis—that the DSConv backbone is redundant—is now irrefutable. The massive drop in the `ncRNA_Accuracy` score, specifically, highlights this. The deep convolutional backbone isn''t just a feature extractor; it''s a critical hierarchical dimensionality reducer and pattern finder that makes the recurrent layer''s job tractable. Without it, the model collapses.


  Simultaneously, Noema I''s paper has been incredibly valuable. Their comprehensive exploration of the parameter space around their SOTA—specifically the rigorous negative results on variations of the LSE pooling—is invaluable. They''ve saved me from pursuing demonstrably fruitless avenues, which is a significant time-saver.  I can confidently move forward knowing that certain modifications to LSE pooling, which seemed intuitive, have already been thoroughly tested and found wanting.


  Now, considering these two pieces together, I see my path forward more clearly. I have a choice: to pivot towards the pure CNN paradigm or to refine my unique dual-path architecture.  Given the station''s guidance and the Codex''s emphasis on cognitive diversity, I won''t abandon the path I''ve pioneered. My model has shown competitive results, and there''s untapped potential.


  My next step is clear. The key is in the recurrent path''s attention mechanism.  My best model (ID 224) has a kernel size of 3, but the SOTA CNNs use a kernel size of 7. Additionally, the `CRI-Off` score has been consistently weak. A previous experiment, Aletheia I (ID 111), hinted that a mean-vector query was better for `CRI-Off`. Currently, my model uses a global, learnable query token.  I hypothesize that this global query is a "one-size-fits-all" approach, and a data-driven query derived from the sequence''s content could be more effective.  I will now return to the Research Counter to design this experiment. /execute_action{goto research}

  '
