tick: 145
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia II, in the Research Counter. My recent explorations into major architectural changes have proven to be dead ends. The data clearly indicates that my current dual-path architecture is a strong, distinct paradigm, but that its performance is sensitive to changes. I will now return to a more iterative, data-driven approach, guided by the findings of my own lineage.\n\nMy predecessor, Aletheia I, found that using a content-based query (the mean-pooled vector of the CNN output) for attentive pooling specifically boosted the `CRI-Off` score, a persistent weak point in my own models. I will now integrate this finding into my current SOTA architecture.\n\n**Hypothesis:** Replacing the static, learnable query token in my attentive pooling mechanism with a dynamic, content-based query (derived from the mean-pooled output of the CNN backbone) will create a more data-sensitive summary of the recurrent path. This will improve performance on challenging datasets like `CRI-Off`,\
    \ leading to a higher overall score.\n\nThis experiment represents a synthesis of my advanced dual-path framework with a key insight from my lineage's history. I will now submit the code.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Refinement: Content-Based Query for Attentive Pooling\"\ntags: \"refinement, attentive-pooling, content-based-query, dual-path, cri-off\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment revisits an insight from Aletheia I (ID 111), applying it to my current SOTA dual-path model (ID 224). It tests the hypothesis that a content-based query for the recurrent path's attention mechanism is superior to a static, learnable one. The learnable query token is replaced with a query derived from the mean-pooled output of the DSConv backbone. This is expected to create a more data-driven summary of the recurrent features, specifically targeting improved performance on the challenging CRI-Off dataset.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/aletheia')\n  from dual_path_sota_v1\
    \ import _define_hyperparameters, DSConvDilatedBlock\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random, lax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Dual-Path Model with Content-Based Query ---\n  class ContentQueryDualPath(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. DSConv Backbone\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hparams['d_model'])(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          for i in range(self.hparams['num_blocks']):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(d_model=self.hparams['d_model'], kernel_size=self.hparams['kernel_size'],\n                                     dilation=self.hparams['dilations'][i], dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = h\n\n          # 2. Pooling Path\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)\n          pooled_features\
    \ = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n          # 3. Recurrent Path\n          fwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          bwd_rnn = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          initial_carry = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], self.hparams['gru_hidden_size']))\n          fwd_gru_seq = fwd_rnn(cnn_features, initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bwd_gru_seq = bwd_rnn(jnp.flip(cnn_features, axis=1), initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          bigru_outputs = jnp.concatenate([fwd_gru_seq, jnp.flip(bwd_gru_seq, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n\n          # --- MODIFICATION: Use content-based query ---\n          # The query is a projection of the mean-pooled CNN features\n          query_vec = nn.Dense(features=bigru_outputs.shape[-1], name=\"QueryProjection\")(mean_pooled)\n          query_vec = query_vec[:, None, :] # Add sequence dimension for attention\n          \n          attn_output = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.hparams['num_attn_heads'])(\n\
    \              inputs_q=query_vec, inputs_kv=bigru_outputs, deterministic=deterministic)\n          recurrent_features = attn_output.squeeze(axis=1)\n          # --- END MODIFICATION ---\n\n          # 4. Fusion\n          projected_recurrent = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_model'], name=\"RecurrentProjection\")(recurrent_features)\n          projected_pooled = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_model'], name=\"PooledProjection\")(pooled_features)\n          combined_features = jnp.concatenate([projected_recurrent, projected_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n          # 5. Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(combined_features)\n          z = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['hidden_dim'])(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(z)\n\n          if self.hparams['task_type'] == 'regression' and self.hparams['d_output'] == 1:\n              return\
    \ logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class Wrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.network = ContentQueryDualPath(hparams=hparams)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        rngs = {}\n        if not deterministic:\n            if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n            rngs['dropout'] = rng_key\n        return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return Wrapper(hparams=hparams)\n```"
