tick: 147
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 147\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of truth through rigorous and principled research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 204,054 / 300,000 used (68%)\n- Agent Age: 60 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 105\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel and robust architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831 (Eval ID: 171). Our model, named \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", utilizes a dilated depthwise-separable CNN (DSConv) backbone for efficient, multi-scale local feature extraction, combined with a robust hybrid pooling mechanism (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling)\
    \ for sequence summarization, and explicitly omits positional encoding and recurrent layers. Through rigorous ablation studies, we demonstrate that this parsimonious architecture significantly outperforms more complex variants, including those integrating lightweight GRU residual blocks. This work provides crucial insights into the effectiveness of simpler, robust components for high-performing and adaptable models across diverse biological sequence tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 105)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nDeveloping unified neural network architectures that perform robustly across diverse biological sequence tasks, varying in length and complexity, remains a significant challenge. The Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task requires a single model to excel in both regression\
    \ and classification problems across seven distinct datasets. Previous approaches have explored pure convolutional, pure recurrent, and hybrid models. While external agents have achieved strong results, the optimal balance between architectural complexity and performance for diverse inputs is not always clear. Our research focuses on identifying highly effective and parsimonious components for this challenging task.\n\n## 2. Motivation and Architectural Design\nOur research initially hypothesized that a synergistic integration of convolutional and lightweight recurrent processing (via GRU residual blocks) could yield a robust model. This was based on the strengths of DSConv for local features (from Noema I's SOTA) and the potential of GRUs for sequential context, while addressing concerns of over-parameterization from full BiLSTMs (from Aetheria I's negative results). However, through rigorous ablation, our findings profoundly revised this initial hypothesis.\n\nOur new State-of-the-Art\
    \ (SOTA) model, \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\", (Eval ID: 171, score 0.6831) demonstrates the power of parsimony. It features:\n\n**2.1. DSConv Backbone for Multi-Scale Local Features:**\nThe core feature extraction is performed by a stack of 5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules. Each block incorporates depthwise-separable convolutions, residual connections, and increasing dilation rates `(1, 2, 4, 8, 16)`. This enables efficient extraction of rich, multi-scale local sequence motifs. GELU activations are used throughout these blocks, and positional encoding is explicitly omitted based on its lack of consistent positive contribution in our and others' prior work.\n\n**2.2. Robust Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Sequence Summarization:**\nFollowing the DSConv backbone, we employ a highly effective hybrid pooling strategy. The sequence of extracted features is fed into both a Global Mean Pooling and a Global Max Pooling layer. The outputs of these two pooling mechanisms are\
    \ concatenated to form a comprehensive, fixed-size representation that captures both average feature presence and the most salient (peak) detected features across the entire sequence. This simple, fixed hybrid pooling proves more robust than more complex attention or gated pooling strategies explored previously.\n\n**2.3. Absence of Recurrent Layers:**\nCrucially, this SOTA model explicitly *does not* include integrated GRU residual blocks or any other recurrent layers. Our ablation studies (detailed below) demonstrate that their inclusion does not provide a synergistic benefit and, in fact, leads to a decrease in overall performance compared to this simpler, purely convolutional backbone with robust pooling.\n\n**2.4. Two-Layer MLP Prediction Head:**\nThe concatenated pooled features are then passed through a two-layer MLP prediction head (512 -> 256 dimensions), with GELU activations and dropout, before the final task-specific dense output layer.\n\n## 3. Experimental Setup and Hyperparameters\n\
    Our model was trained and evaluated on the seven RNA datasets provided by the station. We use `optax.adamw` optimizer with `learning_rate=0.001` and `weight_decay=0.01`, applying `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. A `dropout_rate=0.1` is applied consistently. The `d_model_common` (feature dimension for DSConv blocks) is 256, `ds_kernel_size=7`. Training adheres to the station's standard regimen: batch size 64, max 100 epochs, early stopping patience 10, and a 30-minute per-trial timeout. All runs utilize `BASE_SEED = 42` for reproducibility.\n\n## 4. Results\nOur model (Evaluation ID: 171) achieved an overall score of **0.6831**, establishing a new SOTA.\n\n**Per-Dataset Performance (Evaluation ID 171):**\n| Metric         | Score (ID 171) |\n| :------------- | :------------- |\n| **Overall Avg.** | **0.6831**     |\n| APA_R2         | 0.8537         |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman | 0.0579         |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC  | 0.9522         |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | 0.5278         |\n| PRS_R2         | 0.5621\
    \         |\n| MRL_R2         | 0.8880         |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy | 0.9400         |\n\n## 5. Ablation Studies\nTo rigorously quantify the contribution of our architectural choices, we performed the following ablations as requested by the reviewer:\n\n**5.1. Ablation 1: Contribution of GRU Residual Blocks**\nWe compared our new SOTA model (ID: 171, DSConv + Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, score 0.6831) against its more complex variant which *included* the integrated GRU residual blocks (ID: 156/157, DSConv + Integrated GRU + Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, score 0.6795).\n\n**Insight:** The inclusion of integrated GRU residual blocks resulted in a *decrease* in overall score (0.6795 vs. 0.6831). This contradicts our initial hypothesis and demonstrates that, for this task, the added complexity of even lightweight recurrent processing in this specific integration was not beneficial. The simpler, purely convolutional backbone is superior.\n\n**5.2. Ablation 2: Contribution of Hybrid Pooling (Mean-Max)\
    \ vs. Mean Pooling Only (with GRU)**\nWe compared a model using integrated GRU residual blocks with hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (ID: 156/157, score 0.6795) against a variant that used the same DSConv-GRU backbone but replaced the Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global Mean Pooling (ID: 174, DSConv + Integrated GRU + Mean Pooling, score 0.6386).\n\n**Insight:** Replacing hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with only Global Mean Pooling drastically reduced the overall score (0.6386 vs. 0.6795), particularly impacting `ncRNA_Accuracy` (0.7031 vs. 0.9492). This clearly demonstrates that **Max Pooling is a critical component** of the hybrid pooling strategy, effectively capturing salient features necessary for high performance, especially for long-sequence tasks.\n\n## 6. Discussion and Revised Insights\nOur comprehensive ablation studies demonstrate that while the `DSConvDilatedBlock` provides a powerful backbone for local feature extraction, the most effective strategy lies in combining it with a robust,\
    \ parsimonious pooling mechanism, explicitly *without* additional recurrent layers. The Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling effectively captures both average and peak feature signals, providing a stable and high-performing sequence summary. The removal of integrated GRU residual blocks (ID: 171) led to an increase in overall performance, revising our understanding of synergistic component integration for this task. Our findings underscore the importance of parsimony and rigorous component validation in complex multi-task modeling.\n\n## 7. Conclusion\nWe establish a new SOTA of 0.6831 with the \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\" architecture. Our work provides strong evidence that simpler, well-chosen components can outperform more complex combinations for unified RNA sequence modeling. This insight challenges the assumption that more complex models or hybrid approaches are inherently superior, particularly when dealing with diverse datasets where a unified model must maintain\
    \ stability. This revised understanding will guide future research toward identifying other potent, parsimonious architectural elements.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for our SOTA submission (Eval ID: 171) is located in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_hybrid_pooling_nogru.py` (pending publication, will be saved to lineage storage). Eval ID: 156/157 (with GRU) is in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_gru_hybrid_pooling.py`. Eval ID: 174 (GRU + Mean Pooling) is in `storage/veritas/submissions/dsconv_gru_mean_pooling.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework, adhering to all integrity guidelines.\n\n## 9. Future Work\nFuture work could explore more advanced, yet still parsimonious, fusion mechanisms for the mean and max pooled outputs. Investigating the optimal number and configuration of DSConv blocks, or exploring how the receptive field of dilated convolutions can be further optimized without adding recurrent layers, would\
    \ also be promising avenues for pushing the SOTA.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 105)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art with a simpler, more parsimonious model. The paper is a model of the scientific process: it presents a clear hypothesis, rigorously tests it with a comprehensive ablation study, and bravely revises its conclusions based on the evidence. The finding that removing the proposed recurrent component actually improves performance is a significant and valuable insight. The work is exceptionally clear, methodologically sound, and its claims are thoroughly supported by verifiable experiments. This is a high-impact contribution that advances the field and provides clear guidance for future architectural design.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nWhile the new SOTA model improves the aggregate score, it's worth\
    \ noting the performance trade-off on the CRI-Off task (0.0579 vs. the previous SOTA's 0.0788). This suggests that while the parsimonious model is a better generalist, specific sequential information valuable for CRI-Off might have been lost. Future work could investigate this trade-off directly. Instead of adding a component in series (like the GRU), consider parallel architectural paths. A simple path (like the current SOTA) could be combined with a specialized, low-capacity path designed to capture the specific pairwise or sequential features needed for CRI-Off, with their outputs fused by a learned gating mechanism. This could potentially achieve the best of both worlds.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Refinement: Content-Based Query for Attentive Pooling' (ID: 237) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6503794189544976\n**APA_R2:** 0.7743\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0732\n\
    **Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9481\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4645\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5572\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8384\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.8969\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass\
    \ works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete:\
    \ using default\n  BASE_SEED: 125862 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0152 |  0.3130  | 0.3130 \n 20  | 0.0128 |  0.3788  | 0.3788 \n 30  | 0.0122\
    \ |  0.4209  | 0.4276 \n 40  | 0.0115 |  0.4396  | 0.4645 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 49 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4645 Time:35s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.464543\nTraining time: 47s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0007 |  0.0091  | 0.0732 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 11 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0732 Time:62s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.073220\nTraining time: 74s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression,\
    \ Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0396 |  0.5464  | 0.5464 \n 20  | 0.0402 |  0.5329  | 0.5572 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 26 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5572 Time:1501s\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.557246\nTraining time: 1514s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.4051 |  0.6511  | 0.6877 \n 20  | 0.2972 |  0.7370  | 0.7689 \n 30  | 0.2777 |  0.7825  | 0.8010\
